  <h1>How compound interest works</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Compound interest is interest paid on your original sum and on the interest it has already
    earned. That one sentence explains most of personal finance. Put $10,000 away at 7% a year
    and leave it alone: after 30 years it is worth about $76,123. If only the original $10,000
    had earned interest, the simple-interest answer, it would be $31,000. This page shows the
    formula, works the numbers by hand, and explains the rule of 72 and the one cost that
    matters most: starting late.
  </p>
  <nav class="onpage" aria-label="On this page"><p class="onpage-title">On this page</p><ul><li><a href="#the-short-answer">The short answer</a></li><li><a href="#simple-interest-against-compound-interest">Simple interest against compound interest</a></li><li><a href="#the-formula">The formula</a></li><li><a href="#worked-example-one-a-lump-sum">Worked example one: a lump sum</a></li><li><a href="#worked-example-two-monthly-deposits">Worked example two: monthly deposits</a></li><li><a href="#how-often-interest-is-added">How often interest is added</a></li><li><a href="#the-rule-of-72">The rule of 72</a></li><li><a href="#starting-ten-years-earlier">Starting ten years earlier</a></li><li><a href="#five-mistakes-that-break-the-calculation">Five mistakes that break the calculation</a></li><li><a href="#what-compounding-cannot-do">What compounding cannot do</a></li><li><a href="#questions-people-ask">Questions people ask</a></li></ul></nav>

  <h2 id="the-short-answer">The short answer</h2>
  <p>
    Each period, interest is worked out on the balance as it stands, and the balance already
    contains every earlier period's interest. So the interest added keeps growing even though
    the rate never moves. At 7% a year, $10,000 earns $700 in the first year. The second year's
    interest is 7% of $10,700, which is $749. By year 10 the balance has grown enough that one
    year's interest is about $1,287; by year 30 it is about $4,980, nearly half the original
    sum, earned in a single year without anything new being added.
  </p>
  <p>
    The formula for a lump sum is A = P × (1 + r/n)<sup>nt</sup>, where P is the starting
    amount, r the yearly rate as a decimal, n the number of times a year interest is added, and
    t the number of years. The rest of this page is that formula, taken apart piece by piece.
  </p>

  <h2 id="simple-interest-against-compound-interest">Simple interest against compound interest</h2>
  <p>
    Simple interest pays the same amount every year, because it only ever looks at the original
    sum. Compound interest pays on a balance that grows, so the yearly payment grows too. The
    gap is small at first and then widens quickly:
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th scope="col">Years</th><th scope="col">Compound balance</th><th scope="col">Simple-interest balance</th><th scope="col">Extra from compounding</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>1</td><td class="num">$10,700</td><td class="num">$10,700</td><td class="num">$0</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2</td><td class="num">$11,449</td><td class="num">$11,400</td><td class="num">$49</td></tr>
      <tr><td>3</td><td class="num">$12,250</td><td class="num">$12,100</td><td class="num">$150</td></tr>
      <tr><td>5</td><td class="num">$14,026</td><td class="num">$13,500</td><td class="num">$526</td></tr>
      <tr><td>10</td><td class="num">$19,672</td><td class="num">$17,000</td><td class="num">$2,672</td></tr>
      <tr><td>20</td><td class="num">$38,697</td><td class="num">$24,000</td><td class="num">$14,697</td></tr>
      <tr class="point"><td>30</td><td class="num">$76,123</td><td class="num">$31,000</td><td class="num">$45,123</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    $10,000 at 7% a year, compounded once a year, no deposits added. Between year 20 and year
    30 the compounded balance nearly doubles, while the simple-interest balance rises by only
    $7,000. Time does the heavy lifting: the first ten years add $9,672, the last ten add
    $37,426.
  </p>

  <h2 id="the-formula">The formula</h2>
  <p>
    For a lump sum left alone:
  </p>
  <p>
    A = P × (1 + r/n)<sup>nt</sup>
  </p>
  <p>
    Each letter: A is the final amount, principal plus interest. P is the starting amount. r is
    the yearly rate written as a decimal, so 7% is 0.07. n is how many times a year interest is
    added: 1 for yearly, 4 for quarterly, 12 for monthly, 365 for daily. t is the number of
    years. To find only the interest earned, subtract the starting amount: interest = A − P.
  </p>
  <p>
    For regular deposits, a different formula is needed, because every deposit has a different
    amount of time to grow. With a fixed deposit C made at the end of every period:
  </p>
  <p>
    Future value = C × [((1 + r/n)<sup>nt</sup> − 1) ÷ (r/n)]
  </p>
  <p>
    Our compound interest calculator uses this second formula for the monthly deposit, and the
    first one for the starting amount, with monthly compounding and deposits at month end. The
    worked examples below use the same setup, so you can check them against the calculator.
  </p>

  <h2 id="worked-example-one-a-lump-sum">Worked example one: a lump sum</h2>
  <p>
    $5,000 at 8% a year, compounded quarterly, for 5 years. No deposits added.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th scope="col">Step</th><th scope="col">Working</th><th scope="col">Result</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Convert the rate to a decimal</td><td>8 ÷ 100</td><td class="num">0.08</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Rate per period</td><td>0.08 ÷ 4</td><td class="num">0.02</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Total number of periods</td><td>4 × 5</td><td class="num">20</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Growth factor</td><td>(1.02)<sup>20</sup></td><td class="num">1.485947</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Multiply by the starting amount</td><td>5,000 × 1.485947</td><td class="num">$7,429.74</td></tr>
      <tr class="point"><td>Interest earned</td><td>7,429.74 − 5,000</td><td class="num">$2,429.74</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    The same $5,000 at 8% on simple interest would earn $400 a year, $2,000 over five years,
    and end at $7,000. Compounding quarterly adds $429.74 on top.
  </p>

  <h2 id="worked-example-two-monthly-deposits">Worked example two: monthly deposits</h2>
  <p>
    $200 deposited at the end of every month, earning 7% a year compounded monthly, for 30
    years, starting from nothing.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th scope="col">Step</th><th scope="col">Working</th><th scope="col">Result</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Rate per month</td><td>0.07 ÷ 12</td><td class="num">0.0058333</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Total number of months</td><td>12 × 30</td><td class="num">360</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Growth factor</td><td>(1.0058333)<sup>360</sup></td><td class="num">8.1165</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Deposit formula</td><td>200 × (8.1165 − 1) ÷ 0.0058333</td><td class="num">$243,994.20</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Total paid in</td><td>200 × 360</td><td class="num">$72,000</td></tr>
      <tr class="point"><td>Interest earned</td><td>243,994.20 − 72,000</td><td class="num">$171,994.20</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    The deposits total $72,000. The interest, $171,994, is more than twice what was paid in.
    That is the shape of compounding with regular saving: the deposits matter early, the
    interest takes over late. After 10 years the balance is about $34,617, of which $24,000
    was deposited and $10,617 is interest. After 20 years the balance is about $104,185,
    $48,000 deposited and $56,185 interest.
  </p>

  <h2 id="how-often-interest-is-added">How often interest is added</h2>
  <p>
    Compounding more often helps, but far less than people expect. The rate matters much more
    than the frequency. Take $10,000 held for one year at a 6% nominal rate:
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th scope="col">Compounding</th><th scope="col">After one year</th><th scope="col">Effective yearly rate</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Yearly</td><td class="num">$10,600.00</td><td class="num">6.000%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Monthly</td><td class="num">$10,616.78</td><td class="num">6.168%</td></tr>
      <tr class="point"><td>Daily</td><td class="num">$10,618.31</td><td class="num">6.183%</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Moving from yearly to daily compounding adds $18.31 on $10,000 in a year. Moving the rate
    from 6% to 7% adds $100. When two accounts quote different schedules, compare them with the
    effective annual rate, the APY in consumer banking, which is (1 + r/n)<sup>n</sup> − 1.
    That is the number that already folds the frequency in.
  </p>

  <h2 id="the-rule-of-72">The rule of 72</h2>
  <p>
    Divide 72 by the yearly rate and you get roughly how many years money takes to double. The
    US Securities and Exchange Commission's investor-education site gives the standard example:
    at 9% a year, money doubles about every eight years. The rule is a shortcut, not exact, but
    it is close where it matters most:
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th scope="col">Yearly rate</th><th scope="col">Rule of 72: years to double</th><th scope="col">Exact: years to double</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>4%</td><td class="num">18.0</td><td class="num">17.7</td></tr>
      <tr><td>6%</td><td class="num">12.0</td><td class="num">11.9</td></tr>
      <tr><td>8%</td><td class="num">9.0</td><td class="num">9.0</td></tr>
      <tr><td>9%</td><td class="num">8.0</td><td class="num">8.0</td></tr>
      <tr><td>12%</td><td class="num">6.0</td><td class="num">6.1</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    The exact doubling time is ln(2) ÷ ln(1 + r), with r as a decimal. The rule stays within
    about half a year of exact between 4% and 12%, which covers most savings and investment
    rates people actually meet. Outside that range it drifts: at 2% it says 36 years against an
    exact 35.0, and at 20% it says 3.6 years against an exact 3.8. Use it for a quick sense of
    scale, then use the formula for the real number.
  </p>

  <h2 id="starting-ten-years-earlier">Starting ten years earlier</h2>
  <p>
    The main input to compounding is time, not cleverness. Two people both deposit $200 at the
    end of every month at 7% a year compounded monthly, and both stop at 65. One starts at 25,
    the other at 35:
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th scope="col">Starts at</th><th scope="col">Years of deposits</th><th scope="col">Total paid in</th><th scope="col">Balance at 65</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>25</td><td class="num">40</td><td class="num">$96,000</td><td class="num">$524,963</td></tr>
      <tr class="point"><td>35</td><td class="num">30</td><td class="num">$72,000</td><td class="num">$243,994</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    The early starter pays in only $24,000 more and ends with $280,969 more. To reach the early
    starter's balance by 65, the late starter would have to deposit about $430 a month, more
    than double, for 30 years. No rate you can pick fixes a missing decade: at 10% a year the
    late starter still ends behind the early starter at 7%. This is the one honest argument for
    starting small now rather than waiting until you can afford a bigger deposit.
  </p>

  <h2 id="five-mistakes-that-break-the-calculation">Five mistakes that break the calculation</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Using the percentage as a decimal.</strong> Putting 7 into the formula instead of
      0.07 treats the rate as 700% and the answer explodes. Divide the rate by 100 first,
      every time.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Mixing up n and t.</strong> The exponent is n times t, the total number of
      periods, not the number of years. Monthly compounding for 30 years is 360 periods, not
      30. Using 30 instead understates the result badly.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Using the lump-sum formula for regular deposits.</strong> A = P(1 + r/n)<sup>nt</sup>
      assumes one deposit at the start and nothing after. Monthly saving needs the deposit
      formula in the section above, because every deposit has a different amount of time to
      grow.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Treating the rate as a promise.</strong> The formula assumes the same rate every
      year. Real investments rise and fall, and savings account rates change. A projection is
      a picture of what a steady rate would do, not a forecast of what will happen.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Forgetting inflation, fees and tax.</strong> A balance that doubles in 30 years
      at 7% has not doubled in buying power if prices rose 3% a year over the same period.
      Fees and tax take their cut before the balance is yours. The calculator shows the
      nominal balance; the real value is lower.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="what-compounding-cannot-do">What compounding cannot do</h2>
  <p>
    Compounding runs on debt the same way it runs on savings. Unpaid interest on a card or loan
    is added to the balance, and then interest is charged on that larger balance. At 21% a year,
    a debt doubles in about three and a half years if nothing is paid; at 45%, in under two
    years. The same arithmetic that builds a retirement balance quietly builds a debt
    balance, which is why the first use of the formula most people meet is the expensive one.
  </p>
  <p>
    It also cannot create money from nothing. The formula multiplies whatever rate you feed it,
    so a wrong rate gives a confidently wrong answer. A 7% projection is only as honest as the
    assumption behind the 7%. Check the rate against what the account actually pays, after
    fees, and run the same numbers at a lower rate to see how much the answer depends on it.
    At $200 a month for 30 years, 7% gives $243,994 and 5% gives $166,452: a two-point change
    in the rate moves the answer by $77,542.
  </p>

