  <h2 id="questions-people-ask">Questions people ask</h2>
  <dl class="faq">
    <dt>How do I calculate compound interest by hand?</dt>
    <dd>
      Use A = P × (1 + r/n)<sup>nt</sup>. Convert the rate to a decimal, divide it by the
      number of compounding periods per year, add 1, raise it to the total number of periods,
      and multiply by the starting amount. Subtract the starting amount to get the interest
      earned. For monthly deposits, use the deposit formula in the section above instead.
    </dd>
    <dt>What is the difference between compound and simple interest?</dt>
    <dd>
      Simple interest pays only on the original sum, the same amount every year. Compound
      interest pays on the original sum plus all interest already earned, so the yearly payment
      grows. On $10,000 at 7% for 30 years, simple interest ends at $31,000 and compound
      interest at $76,123.
    </dd>
    <dt>Is monthly compounding much better than yearly?</dt>
    <dd>
      Not much. On $10,000 at a 6% nominal rate for one year, yearly compounding gives
      $10,600.00 and monthly gives $10,616.78. The rate matters far more than the frequency.
      Compare accounts by their effective annual rate, the APY, which already folds the
      frequency in.
    </dd>
    <dt>How accurate is the rule of 72?</dt>
    <dd>
      Within about half a year of the exact doubling time for rates between 4% and 12%. At 9%
      it says 8 years and the exact answer is 8.0 years. It drifts outside that range, so use
      it for a quick sense of scale and the formula for the real number.
    </dd>
    <dt>Does compound interest work on shares, which pay no interest?</dt>
    <dd>
      The same arithmetic applies when returns are reinvested instead of taken out: dividends
      buy more shares, and the growth applies to a bigger holding each year. The difference is
      that share returns are not steady, so the formula shows what a steady average return
      would do, not what will actually happen.
    </dd>
    <dt>Why does my calculator give a different answer from yours?</dt>
    <dd>
      Usually the deposit timing or the compounding frequency. Our calculator compounds
      monthly and treats deposits as made at month end. A calculator that treats deposits as
      made at month start, or compounds yearly, gives a slightly different number from the
      same inputs. Check those two settings first.
    </dd>
  </dl>

